/* ============================================================================
   discounted-payback-period.epher -- how many years until a project's
   DISCOUNTED cash flows recover its cost

   The discounted payback works like the plain payback (the sibling script
   payback-period.epher) but discounts every inflow back to today at the
   cost of capital first: each year k contributes flow_k/(1+r)^k. Because
   later money is worth less, the discounted cumulative total recovers more
   slowly, and the payback lengthens. The recurrence is written out; the
   builtin npv validates the final cumulative total (it is the project's
   npv). Source: standard capital-budgeting practice; see Brealey and
   Myers, Principles of Corporate Finance, chapter 5.

   Accuracy: exact fractions where the decimal repeats, 12 displayed digits
   otherwise. The discount rate r = 8% is the cost of capital.

   Run it from a terminal:
     epher "epher scripts/finance/investing/discounted-payback-period.epher"

   The knobs to edit are in the knob section; everything after it is
   machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- knobs ------------------------------------------------------------------
const invested = 100000     // the project's cost today, USD
const rate = 8%             // the cost of capital (discount rate)
const yearly = {30000, 40000, 40000, 30000}   // inflows at the end of years 1 to 4, USD

// ---- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
def dflow(k) = yearly[k] / (1 + rate) ^ k                                    // year k inflow, discounted to today
def dcum(k) = if k == 1 then dflow(1) - invested else dcum(k - 1) + dflow(k)  // discounted cumulative through year k

/* ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
   Discounted cumulative cash flow through each year. Row 1, -650000/9, is
   the exact fraction for -72222.22...; the display keeps fractions when the
   decimal repeats (epher's exact display). Row 4 is the project's npv. */
for k in 1 to 4 do {k, dcum(k)}
print("npv (the row 4 total), builtin check:", npv(rate, {-100000, 30000, 40000, 40000, 30000}))
// Count the full years on the discounted totals: the loop state is {year,
// discounted cumulative} and runs until the total turns positive.
v = {0, 0 - invested}
while v[2] < 0 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + dflow(v[1] + 1)}
// Payback = last full year plus the fraction of the next year that covers
// the shortfall.
print("discounted payback period:", (v[1] - 1) + (dflow(v[1]) - v[2]) / dflow(v[1]), "years")
/* The discounted payback of 3.28 years beats the plain payback of 2.75
   because the 40000 inflows of years 3 and 4 are worth only 31753.29 and
   22050.90 once discounted. Raise the discount rate far enough and the
   project never pays back at all (its npv turns negative). */
/* ---- expected output ----
= 100000
= 0.08
= {30000, 40000, 40000, 30000}
= {{1, -650000/9}, {2, -27650000/729}, {3, -6175.37976934}, {4, 15875.5158145}}
= npv (the row 4 total), builtin check: 15875.5158145
= {0, -100000}
= discounted payback period: 3.2800512 years
*/
